<?xml version='1.0' standalone='yes'?>
<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN [
  <!ENTITY name      "my-unraid-vgpu-manager">
  <!ENTITY author    "hellomrli">
  <!ENTITY version   "2026.08.29b">
  <!ENTITY launch    "Settings/my-unraid-vgpu-manager">
  <!ENTITY gitURL    "https://github.com/&author;/&name;/raw/master">
  <!ENTITY pluginURL "&gitURL;/&name;.plg">
  <!ENTITY md5       "7dcddd6efbad7fea24579b5adeaaa291">
  <!ENTITY plugin    "/boot/config/plugins/&name;">
  <!ENTITY emhttp    "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;">
  <!ENTITY packages  "/boot/config/plugins/&name;/packages">
]>

<PLUGIN  name="&name;" author="&author;" version="&version;" launch="&launch;" pluginURL="&pluginURL;" min="6.11.5" support="https://github.com/&author;/&name;">

<CHANGES>
##2026.08.29b
- ADD: dual driver series support. The driver repo now publishes BOTH the
  16.x LTS (535.309.01) and the 19.x LTS (580.178.05) merged drivers for each
  Unraid kernel. After detecting the installed GPU model the plugin offers the
  matching series: Pascal cards (P4/P40/P100, dropped from branch 17 on) get
  16.x only, T4-and-newer GPUs get a choice with 19.x recommended, and GPUs
  supported by both get a series picker.
- ADD: "Driver series" setting (auto/16/19) in License &amp; Modules; the series
  is scoped through download, update-check, install and live-update (both
  series can coexist on the flash drive; auto follows the hardware).
- ADD: driver series shown on the status panels (installed series + what the
  hardware supports).
- NOTE: the vgpu_unlock kernel patch is only built into the 16.x series (the
  config magic is 535-specific). Native vGPU cards (P4 etc.) never need it.

##2026.08.29
- FIX: driver package cleanup no longer deletes the other driver type's
  package (nvidia and i915 share the packages/&lt;kernel&gt;/ directory) -
  installing Intel used to wipe the downloaded NVIDIA package, and the other
  way around.
- FIX: the i915 update check parsed the version out of "i915-sriov-&lt;ver&gt;-..."
  with cut -f2 (got "sriov" on both sides), so it never notified - now parsed
  with sed.
- FIX: the docker nvidia runtime is merged into an existing
  /etc/docker/daemon.json via jq instead of overwriting the user's config
  (registry-mirrors etc. survive). The driver package itself no longer ships
  a daemon.json - Unraid regenerates that file at docker service start, and
  nvidia-ctk configures the runtime at install/boot (clean driver builds:
  nvidia-...-6.18.47-Unraid-2 and -6.18.44-Unraid-3).
- FIX: nvidia-gridd now restarts when the license port/FeatureType/UI change,
  not only when the server address changes.
- FIX: VF passthrough binding loads vfio-pci first and binds via
  vfio-pci/bind; drivers_probe would probe every driverless PCI device.
- FIX: a stale /boot/config/modprobe.d/nvidia.conf with the invalid
  "cudahost=1" option (left by the old vgpu-driver plugin) is removed.
- FIX: Drivers tab VFs display showed n/a whenever sriov_totalvfs was set
  (duplicated condition in the template).
- (driver repo) the build now pins SHA256 of the NVIDIA .run files and the
  kernel archive, verifies the committed tools via SHA256SUMS, and skips
  dangling soname links.

##2026.08.21h
- ADD: "Attach vGPU to VM" section. Bind a vGPU mdev to a libvirt VM's
  persistent config via virsh (attach/detach), so the vGPU appears as a
  graphics card inside the VM - the Unraid VM editor cannot list mdev devices
  (they are not PCI devices), which this fills in.
- ADD: operation notes - attach/detach takes effect on the VM's next cold
  start; mdev cannot be hot-plugged.

##2026.08.21g
- CHG: Settings page tabs restyled as plain pagination buttons (no icons,
  no bold highlight) for a cleaner look.

##2026.08.21f
- REMOVE: invalid "options nvidia cudahost=1" from nvidia_setup_config.
  The MERGED driver needs no cudahost/vup_kunlock modprobe params (both are
  unknown to the kernel and ignored); vGPU + host CUDA coexistence comes from
  nvidia + nvidia-vgpu-vfio loading side by side.
- DOC: README updated with the three-tab layout and the merged-driver
  verification result.

##2026.08.21e
- ADD: Settings page split into three tabs:
  1. Drivers - driver status + install/uninstall NVIDIA &amp; Intel
  2. NVIDIA GPU - status, license/modules, vGPU devices, profile_override
  3. Intel i915 SR-IOV - status, VF count, passthrough, boot parameters

##2026.08.21d
- ADD: FastAPI-DLS licensing support. nvidia_apply_license now fetches the
  client token from https://&lt;server&gt;:&lt;port&gt;/-/client-token, writes it to
  /etc/nvidia/ClientConfigToken/client_configuration_token.tok, points
  ClientConfigTokenPath at that directory, creates /var/lib/nvidia/GridLicensing,
  and keeps nvidia-gridd running so Q-series vGPU profiles license correctly.
- ADD: Feature type is now a dropdown (0=vPC, 1=vWS, 2=RTX vWS/vDWS).
- NOTE: mdevctl is now bundled in the NVIDIA driver package (see
  my-nvidia-vgpu-driver) because Unraid ships no mdevctl.

##2026.08.21c
- FIX: Intel i915 SR-IOV driver now loads correctly. The i915-sriov module
  depends on intel_sriov_compat (intel_drm_shim_* symbols), which must be loaded
  FIRST - it now is. Also blacklists the stock i915 and unloads it if it was
  loaded without SR-IOV support, so the replacement takes over.
- FIX: i915 SR-IOV no longer relies on a manual modprobe in /boot/config/go.
  A boot event (emhttp started) now brings up both stacks automatically, and any
  leftover manual "modprobe i915" in go is neutralized (backup kept).
- FIX: i915 driver package now ships a doinst.sh so depmod runs automatically
  after install (was why intel_sriov_compat could not be found).

##2026.08.21b
- FIX: drivers were pre-downloaded on plugin install/update and by the daily
  update check. Now drivers are ONLY downloaded when you click Install on the
  plugin page. Installing/updating the plugin itself never touches your network
  or downloads any driver.

##2026.08.21a
- FIX: settings page was blank (white screen) - a bash-style ${var%:*} string
  was accidentally used inside PHP, causing a PHP parse error. Replaced with
  proper sysfs paths (/sys/bus/pci/devices/). The page now renders correctly.

##2026.08.21
- Unified vGPU manager: NVIDIA vGPU + Intel i915 SR-IOV in one plugin
- Drivers are NOT installed by default - choose Install from the plugin page
  (so systems that don't need vGPU stay untouched)
- NVIDIA: merged driver (vGPU for VMs + host GPU for docker/CUDA), license
  settings, unlock, vGPU device manager all in the plugin page
- Intel: i915 SR-IOV driver, VF count management, vfio-pci binding, syslinux
  boot parameters
- Driver packages come from the dedicated driver repos:
  hellomrli/my-nvidia-vgpu-driver (NVIDIA) and hellomrli/my-i915-sriov-driver (Intel)

##2026.08.20
- Initial release for the merged vGPU driver plugin
- MERGED driver: vGPU host driver + standard NVIDIA driver in one package,
  so the host GPU also works for docker containers (--gpus all), not just VMs
- Driver: NVIDIA vGPU 16.14 (535.309.01) for Unraid kernel 6.18.44
- Target GPU: Tesla P4 (native vGPU support, no unlock needed; unlock option
  kept for consumer GPUs)
- vGPU devices managed from the plugin page (add/remove/start/stop, live
  profile list, VM XML snippets), restored at every boot
- Driver updates install live without a reboot (when no VM holds a vGPU)
- vgpuConfig.xml override support for GPUs dropped from newer driver XMLs
- profile_override.toml editable from the plugin page

</CHANGES>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
rm -f $(ls /boot/config/plugins/&name;/&name;*.txz 2>/dev/null|grep -v '&version;')
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Name="/boot/config/plugins/&name;/&name;-&version;.txz" Run="upgradepkg --install-new">
<URL>&gitURL;/packages/&name;-&version;.txz</URL>
<MD5>&md5;</MD5>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
# LOCAL-FIRST fallback: if the plugin package was placed locally (no GitHub
# hosting yet), install it from the flash drive.
if [ ! -d "&emhttp;" ]; then
  LOCAL_PLUGIN_PKG="$(ls /boot/config/plugins/&name;/&name;*.txz 2>/dev/null | head -1)"
  if [ -n "${LOCAL_PLUGIN_PKG}" ]; then
    echo "---Installing local plugin package $(basename "${LOCAL_PLUGIN_PKG}")---"
    upgradepkg --install-new "${LOCAL_PLUGIN_PKG}"
  fi
fi
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Name="&emhttp;/README.md">
<INLINE>
**Unraid vGPU Manager**

Unified management of NVIDIA vGPU and Intel i915 SR-IOV on Unraid:

- NVIDIA vGPU (merged driver): split a GPU into virtual GPUs (mdev) for VMs,
  while the host keeps full GPU access for docker (--gpus all). License
  settings, unlock and device management all live in the plugin page.
- Intel i915 SR-IOV: split an Intel iGPU into up to 7 VFs for VM passthrough.
  VF count, vfio-pci binding and syslinux boot parameters are managed here.

Drivers are installed on demand from the plugin page - installing this plugin
does not touch your GPU setup until you choose to.
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>

KERNEL_V="$(uname -r)"

#Create settings file if not found and migrate missing keys
if [ ! -f "&plugin;/settings.cfg" ]; then
  echo 'first_installation=true
driver_version=latest
update_check=true
unlock=false
nvidia_installed=false
nvidia_license_server=
nvidia_license_port=443
nvidia_feature_type=0
nvidia_load_uvm=true
nvidia_load_modeset=true
nvidia_load_drm=true
intel_installed=false
intel_vf_number=7' > "&plugin;/settings.cfg"
fi
for key in unlock update_check nvidia_installed intel_installed; do
  if ! grep -q "^${key}=" "&plugin;/settings.cfg"; then
    echo "${key}=false" >> "&plugin;/settings.cfg"
  fi
done
if ! grep -q "^nvidia_series=" "&plugin;/settings.cfg"; then
  echo "nvidia_series=auto" >> "&plugin;/settings.cfg"
fi
if ! grep -q "^nvidia_license_server=" "&plugin;/settings.cfg"; then
  echo "nvidia_license_server=" >> "&plugin;/settings.cfg"
fi
if ! grep -q "^nvidia_license_port=" "&plugin;/settings.cfg"; then
  echo "nvidia_license_port=443" >> "&plugin;/settings.cfg"
fi
if ! grep -q "^nvidia_feature_type=" "&plugin;/settings.cfg"; then
  echo "nvidia_feature_type=0" >> "&plugin;/settings.cfg"
fi
if ! grep -q "^intel_vf_number=" "&plugin;/settings.cfg"; then
  echo "intel_vf_number=7" >> "&plugin;/settings.cfg"
fi

#Move old flat package directory layout to per-kernel directories
mkdir -p "&packages;/${KERNEL_V%%-*}"
if find &packages; -maxdepth 1 -type f -name "*.txz" 2>/dev/null | grep -q . ; then
  mv $(find &packages; -maxdepth 1 -type f -name "*.txz*") "&packages;/${KERNEL_V%%-*}/" 2>/dev/null
fi

#Drivers are NEVER downloaded or installed by the plugin itself - only when
#the user explicitly clicks Install on the plugin page (exec.sh install_nvidia
#/ install_intel call download.sh). Systems that don't use vGPU stay untouched.

#Enable the daily update check
if [ "$(grep "^update_check=" "&plugin;/settings.cfg" | cut -d '=' -f2)" == "true" ]; then
  if ! crontab -l 2>/dev/null | grep -q "&emhttp;/include/update-check.sh"; then
    (crontab -l 2>/dev/null ; echo "$((RANDOM % 59)) $(shuf -i 8-9 -n 1) * * * &emhttp;/include/update-check.sh &amp;>/dev/null 2>&amp;1") | crontab -
  fi
fi

#First installation hint
if [ "$(grep "^first_installation=" "&plugin;/settings.cfg" | cut -d '=' -f2)" == "true" ]; then
  /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/dynamix/scripts/notify -e "Unraid vGPU Manager" -d "Installation successful! Go to Settings -> Unraid vGPU Manager to install a driver and configure your GPUs." -l "/&launch;"
  sed -i '/^first_installation=/c\first_installation=false' "&plugin;/settings.cfg"
fi
echo
echo "----- Unraid vGPU Manager plugin v&version; ready -----"
echo "Drivers are NOT installed by default - use the plugin page to install."

</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>

echo "-----------------------------------------------"
echo "---Uninstalling Unraid vGPU Manager...---"
echo "-----------------------------------------------"
#Stop configured devices and daemons
/bin/bash &emhttp;/scripts/rc.vgpu stop 2>/dev/null
#Remove the update check cron job
crontab -l 2>/dev/null | grep -v "&emhttp;/include/update-check.sh" | crontab -
#Remove plugin related files (the kernel modules stay loaded until reboot)
removepkg &name;-&version;
rm -rf /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;
rm -rf &plugin;
echo
echo "-----------------------------------------------------------------------"
echo "---Unraid vGPU Manager uninstalled, please reboot to unload the drivers---"
echo "-----------------------------------------------------------------------"
echo

</INLINE>
</FILE>
</PLUGIN>
